en released into the supernatant  the supernatant is then collected and  deposited into a microtiter plate where  we are then hybridize it with nano  string detection reagents and read it  out on our encounter platform so in this  way we have built an instrument that is  able to characterize many analytes from  a single tissue slice so the technology  has been built to be able to go up to  800 targets at once we're routinely  running 40 to 50 proteins on a single  tissue section today it is able to  characterize multiple analytes so the  platform works for both protein and for  RNA and so that allows us to look at  where there are differences in the  biology between the two analytes it also  allows us to characterize you know  targets that may be accessible via one  analyte but not the other the that is  generated is highly quantitative because  it's based on the encountered detection  platform it's linear up to six logs of  dynamic range and it's it's its  quantitative binary data so you're just  looking at the presence of these targets  it's very easy to to do the data  analysis and to do the data  normalization and data manipulation  because it's just molecular counting  data and the instrument is highly  sensitive as well we can detect detect  analytes down to the analytes present on  a single cell and we do that by  controlling the paths of the UV light  and the area of the tissue that is  exposed for a UV photo cleavage but the  beauty of it is is the only thing that  touches that tissue is that UV light so  at the end of the experiment you still  have that slide you still have that  sample and can use it and for further  downstream characterization and the  platform is also very quick so you can  compare  up to 20 sections per day so it allows  you to really get a data set around a  large number of samples in a very short  amount of time so this is just one  example of how data generated on the  encounter platform looks okay on the  left side of the slide you can see a  transyl section where we have stained  the germinal centers with ki-67 and the  t-cell zones with c d3 and then we've  selected for different regions of  interest for profiling here and you can  see them outlined with the white boxes  and we're showing you the fluorescent  images for each of those each of those  regions of interest on the right and the  corresponding digital counts that were  associated with the molecular lis  bar-coded antibodies for either Q 67 or  C d3 and you can see that the molecular  data exactly matches what we saw with  the fluorescent data but the beauty of  it is is that there's a lot more  molecular league bar coded antibodies  that are present in this sample then we  could visually resolve with the standard  microscope so in addition to getting  molecular data about T 67 and c d3  you're also getting data about these  other 18 antibodies that are present  within that sample and were  characterized in parallel so to fully  take advantage of this platform we have  built a range of protein content that is  suited for the common applications that  people are using DSP for today so each  of our our protein our protein panels  are built around a core of antibodies  that allows basic immune cell profiling  or basic neural neural cell profiling  and then the content can be further  adapted by the addition of one or more  modules of data that allow more refined  scientific questions to be asked and  then in addition to the modules the  users can also add custom content that  really allows you to develop data around  antibodies that are known to be highly  specific for the research question that  you are trying to ask and the other way  that we build customization into DSP  profiling experiments is with the salon  mechanisms that we use for identifying  the regions of interest for profiling so  today there are three different ways in  which a region of interest can be  identified from a sample we can match it  up to a H&amp;E section 