iling  strategies for each and in doing so  we're able to answer different research  questions that were in line with the  clinical questions that were being asked  through the trial both of these papers  were published in Nature Medicine in  2018 in back-to-back publications and  we've been very fortunate to work with  both of these investigators who are  leading melanoma researchers and so in  melanoma is a particularly interesting  area where as we're special profiling  may be able to play a role in  characterizing responses to  immunotherapy because we know that this  is a tumor type which is highly  responsive to immune checkpoint blockade  and patients who receive checkpoint  blockade can have very good durable long  term control of their tumor however  immunotherapies can be very toxic for  these patients and especially the  combination of the immunotherapies which  has has improved efficacy in in some of  these patients also comes with greater  toxicity so we really want to be able to  develop predictive biomarkers that will  identify those patients who are most  likely to benefit from from the  combination immunotherapies without  experiencing the toxicity and secondly  as as melanoma immunotherapy continues  to progress investigators are starting  to look earlier and earlier in the  course of treatment for applying the  immunotherapy at times when the immune  system may still be more robust and when  there's a greater opportunity to have  very long-term durable benefits for  these patients really before it  progresses to metastatic disease and so  with both of these goals in mind we can  use molecular profiling with the geo mix  platform to begin to characterize  pretreatment tumor biopsies and  understand how the the pre-existing  immune response can benefit can predict  response or can be associated with long  term outcomes from immunotherapy  treatment  so the first study to talk about is the  one from from dr. blank and from Tom  Schumacher this is a s